WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:03.896
(dramatic electronic music)

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.010
- [Daniela] This lab in New York

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:10.210
and others around the world

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.960
are racing to answer one of
the most important questions

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.560
that we have right now.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.810
How well will our current
vaccines protect us

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:20.030
from Omicron, that new
worrisome COVID variant?

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.420
- As we expected, Omicron
is quite resistant.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.080
So it evades detection and inhibition

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.760
by the antibodies we produce,

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.420
either after vaccination
or prior infection.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.030
- [Daniela] However, there's
also good news, a booster.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.890
- Maintains the level of
activity against Omicron.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.920
There should be sufficient
to provide protection.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.910
- [Daniela] The lab at
Rockefeller University is one

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.120
of the leaders doing these
types of experiments.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.820
So I went to get an exclusive look

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.450
at the kinds of tests
virologist need to do,

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.060
to come up with those conclusions,

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.723
and what the results might
mean for us going forward.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:55.224
When I visited virologists,

00:00:55.224 --> 00:00:58.010
Theodora Hatziioannou and Paul Bieniasz,

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.540
were about to finish some key experiments.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.690
Since around Thanksgiving,

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:04.760
their lab has been working
around the clock on Omicron.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.610
They've been testing
antibodies and samples

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:07.680
from three groups.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.380
People who have recovered from COVID,

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.130
some of whom got vaccinated.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.550
People who have gotten
boosters of Pfizer's vaccine

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:17.060
and people who just got a
single dose of the J&amp;J shot.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.890
The testing involves looking
at a key part of the virus,

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.110
plus patient samples like this one,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.190
and then how the antibodies
in those samples interact

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:26.440
with the virus.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.820
- We're basically mimicking
what happens in your body

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.810
when the virus enters your body

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.660
and encounters your antibodies.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.550
- [Daniela] During My visit,

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:36.450
Theodora showed me a kind of heat map

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.660
of antibody virus interactions.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.660
- If it's capable of
inhibiting virus infection,

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:46.070
you will turn the red into
a light orange, green,

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.250
and even blue, if the
plasma is quite powerful,

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:49.083
so you get...

00:01:49.083 --> 00:01:51.060
- [Daniela] Like an alert,
the more red you see,

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.280
the less effective antibodies
are at attacking the virus,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.750
and the more of it that
can get into cells.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.990
That's what happened
when they tested Omicron.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.183
The day after my visit,
they had the results.

00:02:02.183 --> 00:02:03.620
- [Paul] some neutralization, but...

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.240
- [Daniela] Neutralization
or the ability of antibodies

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.240
to keep Omicron from entering cells

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.680
was generally lower in Paul
and Theodora's experiments.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.152
The data corroborates with corporate

00:02:13.152 --> 00:02:15.640
and academic labs have found so far.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.600
That current vaccines may be
less effective against Omicron

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.020
than previous versions of the virus.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.630
Theodora's and other researchers
data point to a future

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:24.980
where boosters are the norm

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.850
and vaccines get tailored
to specific variants.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.730
- It really means you
should get a boosters

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.210
as soon as possible,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.730
and boosters should be extended

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.330
to all age categories, including kids.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:38.400
- [Daniela] So how do the test

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.393
that help guide our vaccine
strategy actually work?

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.530
Researchers start by
building a lab-grown version

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.310
of Omicron spike protein from scratch.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.460
The spike protein is what
helps the virus get into cells.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:55.560
It's also a key target for
virus-busting antibodies.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.170
Fabian Schmidt is the researcher

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.510
in charge of reconstructing
the spike protein.

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:01.780
He gave me a tour of the building process,

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:05.820
starting with an online database
of viral genetic sequences.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.400
- So you can see South African,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.800
and then this is now even
allocated up, Omicron.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.870
- [Daniela] That genetic
information serves as the blueprint

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.500
for Omicron spike.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.620
The researchers use bacterial cells

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.610
to copy that genetic code.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.700
Each one of the dots you
see here represents a clone

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.440
or specimen of spike protein DNA.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.330
The other piece of the puzzle is the shell

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.320
of a completely different virus

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.180
to which they attach the
lab-grown Omicron spike protein.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.780
These are basically neutered viruses.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:35.930
They can get into cells,

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:39.040
but they lack the machinery
to reproduce and spread.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.050
That's key because it's
what allows the researchers

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:43.150
to measure the extent

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.630
to which antibodies can
stop the spike protein

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.990
from getting these fake
viruses to latch onto

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:49.630
and infect cells.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:52.250
In a separate and secured
section of the lab,

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.530
The researchers incubate their fake virus

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:56.920
with patient samples
from vaccinated people

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.430
and people who've had COVID.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:00.570
That liquid has antibodies.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.840
The virus antibody mixture is an added

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.160
to cells that have been
sitting in a warm incubator

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.640
inside these little black containers.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.630
Critically, these cells have the receptor

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.280
that the spike protein binds to,

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.390
allowing the virus to enter.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.170
If the neutered virus gets in,

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.960
it varies a set of instructions
that tells the cells

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:19.510
to make luciferase.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:20.920
That's a protein that glows

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:21.753
and is measured

00:04:21.753 --> 00:04:24.130
by the instrument Theodora
showed us earlier.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.730
More glow means more infection,
which is shown in red.

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.520
That means the antibodies
that were less effective.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:33.530
They compared how well antibodies
prevented Omicron spike

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.470
from helping the virus get into cells,

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.720
relative to the original version of spike.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:38.600
And they found that-

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:40.750
- The antibodies in our body,

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.450
particularly over time,

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.320
appear a really poor at
dealing with Omicron.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.370
- [Daniela] The samples they tested

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.340
from patients span several months,

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.820
from right after they were
vaccinated or infected,

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:53.170
to months later.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:56.010
And we know that antibody
levels tend to wane with time.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.800
Plus none of these people
were infected with Omicron.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.210
When people got boosters, though,

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.470
their antibody levels went up and-

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:06.260
- Do retain sufficient
activity to make us hopeful

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:08.960
that these people will be able to deal

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.600
with Omicron infections.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.730
- [Daniela] Theodora said
that the extra antibodies

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:14.610
that boosters provide
should help protect us

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:18.310
against the most severe outcomes,
including hospitalization.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:21.910
- I still remain worried
that Omicron will transmit

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:23.536
between vaccinated people easier.

00:05:23.536 --> 00:05:26.730
So we still need to have
masks and social distancing

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:28.370
for a little while longer.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:29.440
- [Daniela] In the coming weeks,

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.120
we should have more definitive answers

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.480
about Omicron's real world impact

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.022
from data on how well vaccines protect

00:05:36.022 --> 00:05:37.420
people, not just cells.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.350
Public health experts will be able to see

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:40.550
whether vaccinated people

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:42.720
are getting more breakthrough infections.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.010
Those have tended to be milder, but-

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.330
- What is really driving
evolution and emergence

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:50.870
of the variants is resistant
to our antibody responses.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:52.180
- [Daniela] Theodora and others said

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:54.420
that vaccination rates need to increase

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:57.110
to give the virus fewer chances to infect,

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:00.004
mutate and pose new threats in the future.

00:06:00.004 --> 00:06:03.504
(upbeat electronic music)

